<article class="env-lemma" id="090B">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/090B">Lemma <span data-tag="090B">5.26.7</span>.</a> Let $f : X \to X$ be a surjective continuous selfmap of a Hausdorff topological space. If $f$ is not $\text{id}_ X$, then there exists a proper closed subset $E \subset X$ such that $X = E \cup f(E)$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Pick $p \in X$ with $f(p) \not= p$. Choose disjoint open neighbourhoods $p \in U$, $f(p) \in V$ and set $E = X \setminus U \cap f^{-1}(V)$. Then $p \not\in E$ hence $E$ is a proper closed subset. If $x \in X$, then either $x \in E$, or if not, then $x \in U \cap f^{-1}(V)$. Writing $x = f(y)$ (possible as $f$ is surjective). If $y \in U \cap f^{-1}(V)$ then we would have $x = f(y) \in V$ which is a contradiction with $x \in U$. Hence $y \in E$ and $x \in f(E)$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>